 ### [Generative and Style-based Models](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md)
-Generative and Style-based Models extend the NeRF framework from deterministic scene reconstruction to probabilistic 3D-aware image synthesis. Instead of optimizing a single static radiance field for a specific scene, these methods learn a distribution over 3D scenes, allowing the generation of novel, view-consistent images from latent codes. They distinguish themselves from sibling methods like [Edit-NeRF](9388-edit-nerf.md) or [Instance-NeRF](10611-instance-nerf.md) by treating the scene representation as a generative prior rather than a fixed optimization target, often employing adversarial training or diffusion-based priors to enforce photorealism and structural consistency.
+Generative and Style-based Models extend the NeRF paradigm from reconstructing a single static scene to synthesizing novel, high-fidelity 3D-consistent images from a distribution of scenes. They solve the problem of limited training data and the inability of standard NeRF to generalize across a class of objects (e.g., all faces or all cars) by conditioning the radiance field on a latent code. Unlike standard NeRF, which optimizes weights for a fixed scene, these models learn a mapping from a latent space to the scene geometry and appearance, enabling the generation of new instances. They are distinguished from siblings like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) and [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md) by their explicit use of generative priors (GANs, Diffusion) and style-based conditioning to control appearance attributes and ensure multi-view consistency without requiring explicit 3D supervision [G].
 
